{
 "pmid": "35486341",
 "instructions_version": "extract_v5",
 "reports_individuals_with_tnfaip3_variant": true,
 "relevance_note": "A CNV study of 191 suspected-IEI patients describes one TNFAIP3 carrier with individual features: \"heterozygous deletion of TNFAIP3 in patient-191 is another conclusive molecular diagnosis\".",
 "method_note": "Read the full text and supplement by hand; a short script only assembled the JSON. Only patient-191 is in scope; the other 19 CNV carriers (ICOS, 22q11.2, ATM, etc.) and the rest of the 191-patient cohort have no TNFAIP3 variant and get no rows. Table 1 (per-patient CNVs/SNVs) has only its caption in the Markdown; Tab.S1 and Tab.S2 (clinical phenotypes) are images, so no sex, age, ancestry or further phenotype data for patient-191 could be read. The deletion is described only by size (59.9 kb); no coordinates, breakpoints or HGVS are printed. Parental testing: the paper says only, for the whole study, 'analysis of the parents would have been helpful to evaluate if aberrations occurred de novo or were inherited'; this is not person-specific, so inheritance and parental_testing_statement are 'not reported'. cites_prior_report_of_same_patients is true because patient-005 (ICOS, out of scope) was previously reported [23]; patient-191 is not said to be previously reported. The authors give no disease name for patient-191 (only 'conclusive molecular diagnosis'; HA20 is mentioned generically), so disease_label_as_stated is 'not reported'. Pathogenicity 'pathogenic' from 'ACMG guidelines allow classification of this CNV as pathogenic', which follows the sentence naming patient-191; no ACMG codes printed. Checklist: rosacea counted as observed for the composite 'skin lesions' item; chronic sinusitis not counted as 'recurrent infections' and emphysema not counted as interstitial lung disease. No contradictions found.",
